## 9. Java-style Inheritance Example

### Superclass

```java
public class Animal {
    protected String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating");
    }

    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Some sound");
    }
}
```

### Subclass

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " barks");
    }
}
```

### Key Points

```text
Dog extends Animal
Dog inherits name, eat(), and makeSound()
Dog adds bark()
super(name) calls the Animal constructor
```

---

## 10. Code Reuse

Inheritance avoids repeated code.

### Without Inheritance

```text
Dog class has name, age, eat(), sleep()
Cat class has name, age, eat(), sleep()
Horse class has name, age, eat(), sleep()
```

Same code repeated in many classes.

### With Inheritance

```text
Animal class has name, age, eat(), sleep()
Dog, Cat, Horse inherit from Animal
```

### Benefit

```text
shared code is written once
changes can be made in one place
subclasses can focus on their specific behaviour
```

---

## 11. Specialization

A subclass can specialize the superclass by:

```text
adding new attributes
adding new methods
overriding inherited methods
```

### Example

Superclass:

```text
Vehicle
```

Subclasses:

```text
Car
Bike
Bus
```

Shared:

```text
speed
move()
stop()
```

Specific:

```text
Car: numberOfDoors
Bike: hasBell
Bus: passengerCapacity
```

---

## 12. Method Overriding

Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides its own version of a method inherited from the superclass.

### Superclass

```java
public void makeSound() {
    System.out.println("Some sound");
}
```

### Dog Subclass

```java
@Override
public void makeSound() {
    System.out.println("Bark");
}
```

### Cat Subclass

```java
@Override
public void makeSound() {
    System.out.println("Meow");
}
```

### Mark Scheme Phrase

```text
Method overriding allows a subclass to replace an inherited method with its own implementation.
```

---

## 13. Overriding vs Overloading

These two terms are often confused.

| Feature | Overriding | Overloading |
|---|---|---|
| Meaning | subclass redefines inherited method | same method name with different parameters |
| Inheritance needed? | yes | not necessarily |
| Parameter list | usually same | different |
| Runtime behaviour | supports polymorphism | chosen by parameter match |
| Example | `Dog.makeSound()` replaces `Animal.makeSound()` | `print(String)` and `print(int)` |

### Exam Phrase

```text
Overriding changes an inherited method in a subclass, while overloading uses the same method name with different parameter lists.
```

---

## 14. What Is Polymorphism?

Polymorphism means the same method call can produce different behaviour depending on the actual object type.

### Simple Definition

```text
Polymorphism allows objects of different subclasses to be treated as superclass objects while still using their own overridden methods.
```

### Example

```java
Animal a1 = new Dog("Rex");
Animal a2 = new Cat("Mimi");

a1.makeSound();  // Bark
a2.makeSound();  // Meow
```

The method call is the same:

```text
makeSound()
```

But the behaviour depends on the object type.

### Mark Scheme Phrase

```text
Polymorphism allows the same method call to execute different overridden methods depending on the actual object type at runtime.
```

---

## 15. Dynamic Dispatch

Dynamic dispatch means the program chooses the correct overridden method at runtime.

### Example

```java
Animal animal = new Dog("Rex");
animal.makeSound();
```

Even though the variable type is:

```text
Animal
```

the actual object is:

```text
Dog
```

So Java calls:

```text
Dog.makeSound()
```

### Key Idea

The object type, not only the variable type, determines the overridden method used.

---

## 16. Polymorphism with Arrays / Lists

Polymorphism is useful when storing different subclass objects in one collection.

### Example

```java
ArrayList<Animal> animals = new ArrayList<Animal>();

animals.add(new Dog("Rex"));
animals.add(new Cat("Mimi"));
animals.add(new Dog("Luna"));

for (Animal animal : animals) {
    animal.makeSound();
}
```

Output:

```text
Bark
Meow
Bark
```

### Why Useful?

The loop does not need to know whether each object is a dog or cat.  
It simply calls the shared method.

