## 18. Constructors and `super`

A subclass constructor often calls the superclass constructor.

### Example

```java
public class Animal {
    protected String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);
    }
}
```

### Meaning

```text
super(name)
```

calls the constructor of `Animal`.

### Exam Phrase

```text
The subclass constructor can call the superclass constructor using `super` to initialize inherited attributes.
```

---

## 19. Access Modifiers and Inheritance

Common Java access modifiers:

| Modifier | Access |
|---|---|
| private | only inside same class |
| protected | same class, subclasses, same package |
| public | accessible from other classes |
| default | package access if no modifier is written |

### Inheritance Note

Subclasses inherit private attributes, but they cannot directly access them by name.  
They usually use superclass methods such as getters/setters.

### Safer Design

```text
private attributes
public/protected methods
controlled access
```

---

## 20. Inheritance and Encapsulation

Inheritance should still respect encapsulation.

### Poor Design

```java
public int balance;
```

This allows uncontrolled changes.

### Better Design

```java
private int balance;

public void deposit(int amount) {
    if (amount > 0) {
        balance = balance + amount;
    }
}
```

### Why?

The superclass protects data and provides controlled methods for subclasses or other objects.

---

## 21. Abstract Classes

An abstract class can define shared features but may not be directly instantiated.

### Example

```java
public abstract class Shape {
    public abstract double area();
}
```

Subclasses:

```java
Circle extends Shape
Rectangle extends Shape
```

Each subclass provides its own `area()` method.

### Why Useful?

```text
shared type
common structure
force subclasses to implement required methods
supports polymorphism
```

### Exam Note

Only use this depth if your course includes abstract classes.

---

## 22. Interfaces

An interface defines methods that implementing classes must provide.

### Example

```java
public interface Drawable {
    void draw();
}
```

Classes:

```java
Circle implements Drawable
Button implements Drawable
```

### Difference from Inheritance

```text
extends = class inherits from class
implements = class promises to provide interface methods
```

### Exam Note

Interfaces may be beyond basic SL/OOP, but useful for extension understanding.

---

## 23. Composition vs Inheritance

Composition means a class contains another object.

### Inheritance

```text
Dog is an Animal
```

### Composition

```text
Car has an Engine
```

### Example

```java
public class Car {
    private Engine engine;
}
```

### Comparison

| Inheritance | Composition |
|---|---|
| is-a relationship | has-a relationship |
| subclass extends superclass | class contains another object |
| code reuse through parent class | code reuse through object fields |
| can create tight coupling | often more flexible |

### Exam Phrase

```text
Inheritance should model an is-a relationship, while composition should model a has-a relationship.
```

---

## 24. Advantages of Inheritance

| Advantage | Explanation |
|---|---|
| Code reuse | shared attributes/methods written once |
| Easier maintenance | common changes made in superclass |
| Clear hierarchy | models real-world relationships |
| Specialization | subclasses add or override behaviour |
| Polymorphism | same method call works across subclasses |
| Less duplication | repeated class code reduced |

### Strong Answer Pattern

```text
Inheritance improves code reuse because common attributes and methods can be placed in a superclass and inherited by subclasses.
```

---

## 25. Limitations of Inheritance

| Limitation | Explanation |
|---|---|
| Wrong hierarchy | inheritance can be misused when no is-a relationship exists |
| Tight coupling | subclass depends on superclass design |
| Fragile superclass | superclass changes can affect subclasses |
| Over-complexity | deep hierarchies are hard to understand |
| Less flexible than composition | not all relationships are is-a |
| Overriding errors | subclass method may behave unexpectedly |

### Exam Phrase

```text
Inheritance should be used carefully because inappropriate class hierarchies can make a program harder to maintain.
```

