## 31. Exam-style Questions

### Question 1 [2 marks]

Define inheritance.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Inheritance is an object-oriented feature where a subclass inherits attributes and methods from a superclass.

</details>

---

### Question 2 [2 marks]

Define polymorphism.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Polymorphism allows the same method call to behave differently depending on the actual object type, usually through overridden methods.

</details>

---

### Question 3 [4 marks]

Distinguish between superclass and subclass.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

A superclass is a general class that contains shared attributes and methods. A subclass is a more specific class that inherits from the superclass and may add or override features. For example, `Animal` can be a superclass and `Dog` can be a subclass.

</details>

---

### Question 4 [4 marks]

Explain one advantage of inheritance.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Inheritance supports code reuse because shared attributes and methods can be written once in a superclass and inherited by multiple subclasses. This reduces repeated code and makes common changes easier to maintain.

</details>

---

### Question 5 [4 marks]

Explain method overriding using an example.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides its own version of an inherited method. For example, an `Animal` superclass may define `makeSound()`, while `Dog` overrides it to output `"Bark"` and `Cat` overrides it to output `"Meow"`.

</details>

---

### Question 6 [5 marks]

Explain how polymorphism could be used in a game with `Warrior`, `Mage`, and `Archer` classes.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

`Warrior`, `Mage`, and `Archer` could all inherit from a `Character` superclass with an `attack()` method. Each subclass overrides `attack()` with its own behaviour. The game can store them as `Character` objects and call `attack()` on each one, with the correct subclass version running at runtime.

</details>

---

### Question 7 [5 marks]

Distinguish between inheritance and composition.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Inheritance models an is-a relationship, where a subclass is a type of superclass, such as `Dog` is an `Animal`. Composition models a has-a relationship, where one class contains another object, such as `Car` has an `Engine`. Inheritance reuses code through a parent class, while composition reuses code through contained objects.

</details>

---

### Question 8 [6 marks]

A school system has `Person`, `Student`, and `Teacher` classes. Explain why inheritance is suitable.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Inheritance is suitable because both `Student` and `Teacher` are types of `Person`. Shared attributes such as name, email, and date of birth can be stored in the `Person` superclass. `Student` can add student-specific data such as student ID and grade level, while `Teacher` can add staff ID and subject. This reduces duplicated code and creates a clear class hierarchy.

</details>

